|  from the other court. Instead, the judge based her custody decision in
|  part on the testimony of the visitation supervisor who ran the facility
|  that receives most, if not all, of its referrals from the court. Thus, in substituting the mother’s own personal social network for the ad hoc social
|  networks bred by the courts, the supreme court was acting de facto as a
|  family court, wherein witnesses are commonly drawn from the facilities
|  that service the courts. This was not surprising since women of color,
|  often seen as downtrodden despite their educational and professional
|  achievements, are treated in the state supreme court in the same demoralized way that family courts mistreat women: their social networks
